title: Artifact Synthesis

files:
  - .assets/img-103-03-01.png
  - .assets/img-103-03-02.png
  - segger.arm.sandbox:segger.arm.distro.sandbox/BuildC.em.ts
  - segger.arm.sandbox:segger.arm.distro.sandbox/StartupC.em.ts
  - .assets/img-103-01-02.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Its Unique Position}

      The anchor of every distro, you'll never encounter an **EMS** source file which _explicitly_ references {[cu]BuildC}.

      Why&thinsp;??&ensp; Because the **EMS** translator automatically brings {[cu]BuildC} into the mix when building any program.

      Said another way: &nbsp; all `.em.ts` source files _implicitly_ con&shy;tain an {[ck]import}&hellip;{[ck]from}{[cs]'@$distro/BuildC.em'} directive.

      <div class="em-info">

      {[cu]BuildC} itself contains multiple {[ck]import} directives, each bringing additional units into the **EMS** working set.

      </div>

      Before moving ahead, adjust {[em]Board} and {[em]Setup} to once again select our skeletal `segger.arm.sandbox` distro.

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,playground]&thinsp;A Distro Playground}

      As a rule, _every_&thinsp; **EMS** distro package will contain a {[cu]BuildC} com&shy;posite &ndash; including the skeletal distro illustrated here.

      Stripped to its essentials, `segger.arm.sandbox` provides a "playground" for dissecting the **EMS** build flow in isolation.

      <div class="em-info">

      Our <code>..sandbox</code> distro even lacks its own {[cu]BoardC} for configuring a target MCU platform.

      </div>

      You've already seen the target C++ project generated by the `segger.arm.sandbox` distro {[tr,103/02/05]}.

      So let's finally move upstream to {[cu]BuildC} &ndash; as well as ex&shy;amine other source files found in our distro playground.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 A 2
      - mark 3 B 16
      - mark 3 C 27
    focus: [3, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,eyeglasses_2]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Composite Overview}

      The directive at {[bm,A]}&thinsp; declares {[cu]BuildC} as a **Composite** &ndash; the third of four possible roles an **EMS** unit can assume.

      <div class="em-info">

      We first introduced **Composites** in an early tour &thinsp;{[tr,100/03/09]} using the ever-present {[cu]BoardC} as its exemplar.

      </div>

      Unlike the more prevalent **Module** &thinsp;{[tr,100/03/02]}, a **Composite** _only_ exists in the **meta** domain during program build.

      As such, {[cu]BuildC} can define intrinsic meta-functions like {[cs]em$configure}{[bm,B]} outside of a <code class="cmd-cr">namespace</code>**`em$meta`** block.

      The {[cu]BuildC} composite also features {[cs]em$generate}{[bm,C]} &ndash; an intrinsic meta-function for synthesizing build-time files.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 1 4
      - mark 3 2 6
      - mark 3 3 8
      - mark 3 4 9
      - mark 3 5 10
    focus: [3, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,conversion_path]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Generating C++ Code}

      Using their own {[cs]em$generate} meta-functions, several units imported by {[cu]BuildC} synthesize C++ source files:

      {[bm,1]}&thinsp; {[cu]ArmStartupC} &rarr; `em.arch.arm/startup.cpp`<br>
      {[bm,2]}&thinsp; {[cu]IntrVec} &rarr; `em.arch.arm/intr.cpp`<br>
      {[bm,3]}&thinsp; {[cu]REGS} &rarr; `segger.arm.distro.sandbox/REGS.hpp`<br>
      {[bm,4]}&thinsp; {[cu]StartupC} &rarr; `..distro.sandbox/startup.cpp`<br>
      {[bm,5]}&thinsp; {[cu]TargC} &rarr; `emscript.hpp`<br>

      The `main.cpp` program corresponding to {[cu]Gist00} will then (in-)directly {[ck]#include} each of these files {[tr,103/02/04]}.

      <div class="em-info">

      You might want to re-build {[cu]Gist00} {[tr,103/02/02]}, open its `main.cpp` translation, and use **F12** to visit these files.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 4
    focus: [4, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,conversion_path]&thinsp;%[cu]StartupC% &ndash; Generating <code>startup.cpp</code>}

      To illustrate {[cs]em$generate} in action, let's examine how the distro's {[cu]StartupC} composite generates `startup.cpp`.

      Imported by {[cu]BuildC}, **EMS** invokes this {[cu]StartupC} meta-function during its regular program translation process.

      Most {[cs]em$generate} definitions leverage {[cs]$outfile} &ndash; an intrinsic utility only available in the **EMS** meta-domain.

      The object returned by {[cs]$outfile} supports multiple meth&shy;ods for accumulating textual content &ndash; such as {[cf]addFrag}.

      The {[cf]close} method then writes all of this text to the file path originally handed to {[cs]$outfile}.

      <div class="em-info">

      Note that {[cs]$outfile} resolves all output file paths rela&shy;tive to the `workspace/.out` folder.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 A 28
      - mark 3 B 38
      - mark 3 C 47
      - mark 3 D 90
      - mark 3 E 100
    focus: [3, 31]
    text: |
      {[ht,conversion_path]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Capturing Properties}

      An {[cs]em$generate} meta-function will sometimes _interpo&shy;late_ textual content when synthesizing an artifact.

      {[cu]BuildC} uses the {[cs]$property} intrinsic {[bm,B]} to fetch named property values &ndash; with defaults returned when absent.

      <div class="em-info">

      The `props.json` artifact viewed earlier {[tr,103/02/06]} mirrors the build-time bindings accessible via {[cs]$property}.

      </div>

      With full **TypeScript** available to {[cs]em$generate}, line {[bm,C]}&thinsp; in&shy;terpolates {[cn]tools} within the template passed to {[cf]addFrag}.

      Scrolling down, you'll find interpolations of {[cn]opt}{[bm,D]} and {[cn]libflav}{[bm,E]} within this synthesized `build.sh` script.

      <div class="em-info">

      We'll cover {[cu]LinkerC}.{[cf]genScript}{[bm,A]} at the next stop. 

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 A 28
      - mark 3 B 38
      - mark 3 C 47
      - mark 3 D 90
      - mark 3 E 100
      - mark 3 F 113
      - mark 3 G 118
    focus: [3, 93]
    text: |
      {[ht,conversion_path]&thinsp;%[cu]BuildC% &ndash; Generating Scripts}

      You've undoubtedly noticed the <b><code class="cmd-cs">|&thinsp;-&thinsp;&gt;</code></b> prefix on each line of the **TypeScript** template literal passed to {[cf]addFrag}.

      <div class="em-info">

      To improve client readability, {[cf]addFrag} uses this prefix to mark the "left margin" within its synthesized output.

      </div>

      After finalizing `build.sh` {[bm,F]}, {[cu]BuildC}.{[cs]em$generate} syn&shy;thesizes the small `load.sh` script viewed earlier {[tr,103/02/05]}.

      Given `load.sh` has only one line, {[cs]em$generate} defers to a simpler {[cf]addText} method {[bm,G]} to source its content.

      Finally, {[cu]BuildC} controls the synthesis of `linkcmd.ld` by calling the {[cu]LinkerC}.{[cf]genScript} meta-function back at {[bm,A]}.

      &ensp;💡&thinsp;Use **F12** to inspect {[cf]genScript} inside {[cu]LinkerC}.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Visit the {[cn]out}.{[cf]addFile} method used inside {[cu]TargC}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} How do {[cu]ArmStartupC}&middot;{[cu]IntrVec} support {[cu]StartupC}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Compare {[cu]BuildC} with its counterpart in other distros.

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 R 8
    focus: [3, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      &ensp;🔓&ensp;_Whew &ndash; you can finally release that seatbelt&thinsp;!!_

      Once again, nothing happens without {[cu]BuildC} &ndash; which generates artifacts required to build **EMS** programs.

      While our `..sandbox` distro lacks target content (hence no {[cu]BoardC}), its {[cu]BuildC} tracks those found in full distros.

      Pivoting towards {[cu]BoardC}, you'll next learn how {[cu]REGS}{[bm,R]} supports platform-specific **EMS** code found in full distros.

      &ensp;🔒&ensp;_So buckle up, because here we go again&thinsp;!!_

      {[hc]}
